  "description": "The synthetic pin at a steady 20 kW/m with its 300 K coolant at 30 kW/m2/K (the starting state is that steady state). At 2 s the pumps trip: the flow coasts down with a 5 s half time, so the film coefficient follows flow^0.8 (ASSUMED exponent, the usual turbulent-flow scaling) down to a floor of 15 % of its value (natural circulation, also ASSUMED), and the coolant temperature rises 60 K over the minute (the heat is carried off more slowly). The reactor scrams at 3 s: the heat rate falls to an ASSUMED 7 % in 1 s and then decays (1.2 kW/m at 20 s, 0.6 kW/m at 60 s). The history is a handful of straight pieces through those assumed curves, not a thermal-hydraulic calculation. Synthetic heat capacities; thermal only; the power and boundary histories are inputs.",
